## Project context

Both codelines in scope (mock-a and mock-b) use **vitest** with the standard
`describe` / `it` / `expect` idiom.  Assertions are typically `expect(value).toBe(...)`
or `expect(value).toContain(...)`.

### Boundary trap (critical for both tickets)

Both bugs in this project are **off-by-one boundary errors**:

- **MOCK3-1 (mock-a `src/fares.ts`):** `fareFor` uses `rider.age > 65` instead of
  `>= 65`, so a rider aged *exactly* 65 is charged the base fare instead of the
  concession fare.  A test that asserts age 70 (or any age strictly greater than 65)
  receives the concession fare will **still pass** with the buggy code and therefore
  does **not** cover the requirement "riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult
  fare."  Only a test that passes `age: 65` and asserts the expected fare covers it.

- **MOCK3-2 (mock-b `src/schedule.ts`):** `formatStops` loops to
  `stops.length - 1`, silently dropping the final stop.  A test that asserts the
  output contains the first stop, or contains the `->` separator, will **still pass**
  with the bug and does **not** cover "the departure board omits the final stop."
  Only a test that asserts the last stop's name appears in `formatStops` output
  covers it.

When judging coverage, verify that the assertion would actually **fail** under the
buggy implementation — not merely that the test touches nearby values.
